  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Boomke Wies. 4-player Belgian Whist. Fixed partnerships. Bid 7-13 tricks (Boom = 13). No suit named in bidding â€” winner leads first trick and its suit = trump. Scoring: 7-9 tricks = 1pt, 10-12 = 2pt, 13 = 3pt, Boom success = 5pt, Boom fail = -5/+5. Meetje = no-bid fallback. Follow suit, no heading obligation.',
    Player: 'Three players passed. As dealer, you hold Aâ™  Kâ™  Qâ™  Jâ™  10â™  9â™  8â™¥ 7â™¥ 6â™¥ 5â™¥ 4â™¥ 3â™¥ 2â™¥. You have 6 spades headed by the ace! Do you bid 7 (safe, same score as 8-9), go for Boom (all 13 = 5 points, needing 7 more tricks with hearts), or call Meetje (1 point, guaranteed)?',
    objective: "Win tricks according to your team's bid, or prevent the opposing team from making theirs.",
    gameplay: "Players bid in order from dealer's left; lowest bid 7, highest Boom (13). Pass or bid higher; once you pass you may not bid again. When three have passed, dealer must bid 7, bid Boom, or declare Meetje. Highest bidder leads to the first trick; the suit of that card is trump. Each trick: follow suit if possible; no obligation to beat or trump when void. Trick won by highest trump in it, or highest of led suit if no trump. Winner of each trick leads to the next. After 13 tricks, score by bid and tricks won; then next hand or end of round.",
    keyRules: [
      'Bid a number of tricks (7-13); highest bidder picks trump by leading.',
      "No suit mentioned in bidding â€” partners can't know each other's preferred trump.",
      'Boom (13) is the only bid above 9 worth making when three players passed (same score for 7-9).',
      'Meetje: no-bid fallback where dealer leads and majority wins.',
      'Follow suit required; no obligation to head or trump when void.',
